<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yazaman al-Khadim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/891">891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yazaman al-Khadim was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> commander who governed the frontier city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from 882 until his death in 891. He led repeated land and sea raids against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for a time before accepting their authority. He died of wounds received during a campaign into Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and is remembered as one of the most successful leaders of the Tarsus frontier.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Yazaman, also written Yazman, first appears in September or October 882 as a servant (mawla) of al-Fath ibn Khaqan. He was attacked and imprisoned by Khalaf al-Farghani, governor of the Cilician borderlands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a>, the autonomous ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> freed him, rejected Tulunid rule and made him their leader under Abbasid allegiance. When Ibn Tulun marched on the city, its inhabitants opened the sluice gates and flooded the plain, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> having achieved nothing.</p><p>In 883 a large Byzantine army under the Domestic of the Schools, Kesta Stypeiotes, marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>. On the night of 11 September Yazaman surprised its camp at Bab Qalamyah, some 12 km from the city; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> scattered, Stypeiotes and the strategoi of Anatolikon and Cappadocia were killed and much booty was taken. A naval attack of 30 large ships on the fortress of Euripos (Chalkis) soon after was beaten off with heavy loss by Oineiates, governor of Hellas. According to the historian al-Tabari, Yazaman raided as far as al-Maskanin early in 886 and returned with booty and captives, and a naval raid, probably that summer, took four Byzantine ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 890 Yazaman swore allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunids</a> under Ibn Tulun's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khumarawayh">Khumarawayh</a>. In October 891 he raided Byzantine territory with the Tulunid officer Ahmad ibn Tughan al-Ujayfi and besieged the fortress of Salandu, where on 22 October he was hit by a stone from a catapult. The siege was broken off, and he died the next day on the way back. He was buried at the Gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">Jihad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> and succeeded by Ahmad ibn Tughan; the city stayed under Tulunid control until 897.</p><p>Muslims counted him among their most valiant champions, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-al-aqta">Umar al-Aqta</a> and Ali al-Armani, and the Arab sources praise the boldness of his sailors. The 10th-century writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">al-Mas'udi</a> recorded a Greek convert's report that Yazaman's portrait was among those of ten Muslims displayed in Byzantine churches in recognition of their valour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yazaman al-Khadim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Commanders such as <b>Yazaman al-Khadim</b> and Thamal al-Dulafi led land and naval raids into Anatolia and the Aegean, and prisoner exchanges with Byzantium were held on the nearby Lamos river.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-tarsus">Emirate of Tarsus</a></li></ul></section>
